//? Title: Lenstra elliptic-curve factorization
//? Subtitle: Actual failed inverses, original factors and bounded stage1 limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Elliptic-curve factorization finds a divisor when modular point arithmetic needs
an inverse whose denominator shares a proper factor with the composite modulus.
We begin with an actual checkable failed inverse, retain the original composite
inputs, inspect real seeded curve/denominator evidence and distinguish a uniform
smoothness proxy from measured per-curve success. This unsigned64 implementation
is bounded affine stage1 ECM, not arbitrary-precision production ECM or a
primality certificate.
)

\toc

> A useful failure of division

On y^2=x^3+a*x+b modulo n, adding distinct finite points uses slope
lambda=(y_Q−y_P)/(x_Q−x_P). An inverse exists only when gcd(x_Q−x_P,n)=1. A
proper GCD1<g<n directly factors n. Doubling uses numerator3*x_P^2+a and
denominator2*y_P. Infinity and additive-inverse cases use their own shortcuts.

For n91, a12, b1, both P=(0,1) and Q=(7,8) lie on the curve: Q’s right-hand side
is428, congruent64. The actual denominator7 has gcd(7,91)=7, so point addition
exposes the factor before inversion. This is a library arithmetic call with
validated on-curve inputs, not a narrated fictional curve search.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ecm_inverse.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto e = dm::algorithms::ecm_add(91, 12, 1, {0, 1, false}, {7, 8, false});
    std::print("| actual modulus | actual numerator | actual denominator | actual denominator gcd | actual " "inverted | actual factor | exact divisor check |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: " "| --- |\n| 91 | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",
               e.numerator, e.denominator, e.gcd, e.inverted, e.factor,
               91 % e.factor == 0);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d;
    d.node("points", {0, 0}, "Actual on-curve operands\nP(0,1); Q(7,8)").node(
        "den", {6, 0},
        std::format("Actual slope denominator\n{}", e.denominator)).node(
        "gcd", {12, 0},
        std::format("Actual failed inverse\ngcd({},91)={}", e.denominator, e.gcd)).edge("points", "den").edge("den", "gcd").save_svg("build/ecm_inverse.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| actual modulus | actual numerator | actual denominator | actual denominator gcd | actual inverted | actual factor | exact divisor check |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- | ---: | --- |
| 91 | 7 | 7 | 7 | false | 7 | true |
\image(figures/ecm_inverse.svg)
// result_end
\caption(An actual noninvertible denominator exposes the factor7 of91.)
\alttext(The checked native curve addition records the true operands, denominator and GCD; no inverse is attempted after the proper divisor is found.)

> Stage1 and the original composite inputs

Each seeded attempt chooses x,y,a and computes b=y^2−x^3−a*x modulo n. A
discriminant GCD can already reveal a factor; GCD n discards the curve.
Otherwise stage1 successively multiplies the current point by the largest power
of each prime not exceeding bound B. The default preserves B300, at most3000
attempts and the historical xorshift64 seed/order, preceded by small trial
primes through47. The method stops at the first proper factor. No stage2 is
implemented.

Retain the original1517,10403,1000003*101,104729*1009 and999983*3 inputs. Some
are handled by trial division, so their results are explicitly not counted as
evidence of an elliptic-curve inverse failure. The figure counts actual
attempted curves rather than elapsed time or a theoretical runtime estimate.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ecm_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| original n | actual returned factor | actual proper factor | quotient | exact divisor | " "actual small-prime checks | actual curves | actual denominator gcds |\n| ---: | ---: | --- " "| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    std::size_t i = 0;
    for (std::uint64_t n : {1517ULL, 10403ULL, 1000003ULL * 101,
                            104729ULL * 1009, 999983ULL * 3}) {
        auto a = dm::algorithms::lenstra_ecm_analyze(n);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", n, a.factor,
                   a.proper, n / a.factor, n % a.factor == 0,
                   a.work.trial_divisions, a.work.curves,
                   a.work.denominator_gcds);
        x.push_back(i++);
        y.push_back(a.work.curves);
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual curves", {37, 99, 235}, 6).title(
        "Actual curve attempts on the original composites").x_label(
        "original composite index").y_label("actual random curves generated").save_svg("build/ecm_original.svg");
}
```
