> Smoothness is explanatory, not an empirical success probability

Modulo an unknown prime factor p, the elliptic-curve group order lies in the
Hasse interval

$$p+1-2\sqrt p\le\#E(\mathbb F_p)\le p+1+2\sqrt p.$$
\alttext(The number of points on an elliptic curve over a prime field lies near p plus one within two square roots of p.)

Stage1 uses M=lcm(1,...,B), represented by the product of the prime powers.
Having point order dividing M can make a point vanish modulo one prime factor
before another. A B-smooth group order is suggestive, but smoothness alone does
not guarantee divisibility by M: an individual prime power can exceed B. Curve
orders are not uniformly distributed across the interval, point orders can be
smaller, and affine exceptional cases matter. Therefore the following original
B50 interval experiment is only a uniform integer smoothness proxy. It does not
measure ECM success rates or prove a monotone success curve.

The native helper completely validates these small primes and trial-factors
every interval integer; no report-side smoothness algorithm remains. The
original eight target primes are retained.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/ecm_smoothness.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| original prime | actual inclusive lower | actual inclusive upper | exact50-smooth integer " "count | exact interval count | uniform integer fraction, not success rate |\n| ---: | ---: " "| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (std::uint32_t p : {101, 211, 401, 809, 1601, 3203, 6421, 12809}) {
        auto h = dm::algorithms::ecm_hasse_smoothness(p, 50);
        double f = double(h.smooth) / h.total;
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {:.12f} |\n", p, h.lower,
                   h.upper, h.smooth, h.total, f);
        x.push_back(std::log2(p));
        y.push_back(f);
    }
    auto plot = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.points(x, y, "uniform interval fraction", {5, 150, 105}, 6).y_limits(
        0, 1.3).title("Original B50 uniform-integer smoothness proxy").x_label(
        "log2(prime p)").y_label("fraction of interval integers, not ECM success").save_svg("build/ecm_smoothness.svg");
}
```
